# NX789J - the parts of stock's vendor init that this port needs but does not
# ship.
#
# Stock's /vendor/etc/init/hw/ files are reached through init.qcom.rc, which AOSP
# imports as init.${ro.hardware}.rc. This port ships its OWN minimal init.qcom.rc
# (rootdir/etc/init.qcom.rc), which imports stock's init.qti.kernel.rc and - since
# build 17 - stock's init.target.rc verbatim. Files directly in /vendor/etc/init/
# are parsed automatically, which is why this one lives there.
#
# DO NOT re-add here what stock init.target.rc now provides, or it runs twice:
#   on init    wait + symlink /dev/block/bootdevice
#   on fs      mount_all /vendor/etc/fstab.qcom --early, persist chown/chmod
#   on late-fs mount_all /vendor/etc/fstab.qcom --late
#   services   vendor.pd_mapper, vendor.per_mgr, vendor.per_proxy, vendor.mdm_launcher
# Builds 8-16 carried hand-written copies of the mount_all lines and the symlink;
# they were removed when init.target.rc was shipped. The notes on WHY those were
# needed are kept below, beside the one thing still done here (the mkdirs).

# USB gadget (booted system).
#
# AOSP's init.usb.configfs.rc only CONFIGURES a gadget - it writes functions and
# UDC and assumes /config/usb_gadget/g1 with its strings and configs/b.1 already
# exist. Nothing in AOSP creates them; on a QTI device that is stock's
# vendor/etc/init/hw/init.qcom.usb.rc, which this port does not ship. So without
# this block sys.usb.configfs stays 0 (init.usb.rc's "on init" default) and USB
# falls back to the legacy /sys/class/android_usb path, which does not exist on
# this SoC - no adb, no MTP, no enumeration at all.
#
# Modelled on stock's init.qcom.usb.rc, with two deliberate differences:
#   - stock runs "exec u:r:vendor_qti_init_shell:s0 -- /vendor/bin/init.qcom.usb.sh"
#     to derive vendor.usb.controller. That seclabel does not exist in this
#     port's (empty) vendor sepolicy, so the exec would fail. The bootloader
#     already gives us the same value as ro.boot.usbcontroller
#     (=a600000.dwc3, confirmed in the recovery log), so use it directly.
#   - only ffs.adb is set up. MTP/PTP/RNDIS/mass_storage come later.
#
# configfs itself is mounted by AOSP's init.rc, so it is not mounted again here.
on post-fs-data
    mkdir /dev/usb-ffs 0775 shell system
    mkdir /dev/usb-ffs/adb 0770 shell system
    mount functionfs adb /dev/usb-ffs/adb uid=2000,gid=1000,rmode=0770,fmode=0660

on boot
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1 0770 shell shell
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/strings/0x409 0770
    write /config/usb_gadget/g1/bcdUSB 0x0200
    write /config/usb_gadget/g1/strings/0x409/serialnumber ${ro.serialno}
    write /config/usb_gadget/g1/strings/0x409/manufacturer ${ro.product.manufacturer}
    write /config/usb_gadget/g1/strings/0x409/product ${vendor.usb.product_string}
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/functions/ffs.adb 0770 system usb
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs/b.1 0770
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs/b.1/strings/0x409 0770
    write /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs/b.1/MaxPower 900
    setprop sys.usb.controller ${ro.boot.usbcontroller}
    setprop sys.usb.configfs 1

# (System-boot diagnostics moved to /system_ext/etc/init/nx789j-diag.rc in build 17
# so they survive pairing this system with a STOCK vendor image.)

# Mount the vendor fstab. THIS IS WHAT BLOCKED BOOT AFTER THE init_boot FIX.
#
# fstab.qcom has three classes of entry:
#   first_stage_mount  - system/vendor/product/odm/*_dlkm/metadata, done by
#                        first-stage init before this file is even parsed
#   latemount          - /data only, done by "on late-fs: mount_all --late" below
#   neither            - /vendor/firmware_mnt, /vendor/dsp, /vendor/bt_firmware,
#                        /mnt/vendor/persist, /mnt/vendor/qmcs, /mnt/vendor/spunvm
#
# That third group is mounted by an explicit mount_all, which stock does in
#   vendor/etc/init/hw/init.target.rc:  on fs -> mount_all /vendor/etc/fstab.qcom --early
# and this port ships neither init.target.rc nor the init.qcom.rc that imports it.
# So nothing ever mounted them, and /vendor/firmware_mnt in particular holds the
# trustlet images qseecomd loads. Observed chain, from the build-8 dump:
#   no /vendor/firmware_mnt
#     -> vendor.qseecomd exits 255
#       -> KeyMint HAL has no TEE
#         -> keystore2 never registers android.security.maintenance
#           -> vold blocks on it forever (one retry per second, from t=1.17s)
#             -> /data never mounts
#               -> no zygote, no surfaceflinger, OEM splash stays up
#
# --early matches stock: mount everything EXCEPT the latemount entries; /data is
# the --late mount. Both mount_all calls now come from stock init.target.rc.
#
# The mount points for the "--early" group. Done in on init rather than on fs:
# stock init.target.rc is reached through an import from init.rc, so its "on fs"
# mount_all is registered - and therefore runs - BEFORE any "on fs" in this file.
# /mnt itself exists by on init (first-stage tmpfs).
on init
    mkdir /mnt/vendor 0755 root root
    mkdir /mnt/vendor/persist 0771 root system
    mkdir /mnt/vendor/qmcs 0771 root system
    mkdir /mnt/vendor/spunvm 0771 root system

# Mount /data. THIS IS WHAT REBOOTED THE DEVICE 5 SECONDS IN ON BUILD 13.
#
# An earlier version of the comment above claimed AOSP does this itself in
# on late-fs. It does not - system/core/rootdir/init.rc only triggers late-fs
# and then post-fs-data ("Now we can mount /data..."); the actual mount_all
# --late is the DEVICE's job. Stock does it in
#   vendor/etc/init/hw/init.target.rc:93   on late-fs -> mount_all ... --late
# which this port does not ship. So /data was never mounted by anything.
#
# Earlier builds never noticed, because they died before post-fs-data: KeyMint
# was missing, so vold blocked forever on keystore2. Once the TEE chain came up
# (build 13: QSEECOM DAEMON RUNNING, IKeyMintDevice registered, keystore2
# "Successfully registered Keystore 2.0 service"), init sailed through late-fs
# into post-fs-data with /data still unmounted and ran
#   exec - system system -- /system/bin/vdc keymaster earlyBootEnded
# keystore2 then opened its database:
#   E keystore2: open(/data/misc/keystore/persistent.sqlite) ... unable to open
#   E keystore2: panicked at globals.rs:187:14: Failed to open database
# keystore2.rc is "critical window=0", so repeated crashes rebooted the device
# within seconds - the 3-5 s splash-then-reboot.
#
# Ordering: init.rc is parsed first, so its own on late-fs actions (including
# class_start early_hal, which brings up KeyMint) run before this one - the
# same order as stock. fstab's userdata entry needs KeyMint for
# metadata_encryption and is "formattable", so a zeroed userdata is formatted.
# (the "on late-fs: mount_all --late" that lived here is now stock init.target.rc:93)
